# Name: ATASCII
#
# Atari 8-bit (400/800/XL/XE) character set, the safely mappable subset.
# Source: Wikipedia "ATASCII" (raw table + its separate control-character
# list, both cross-checked directly).
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ATASCII
#
# Deliberately NOT included, same reasoning as the built-in CP437's
# 0x00-0x1F scope reduction (TODO.md Abschnitt 6.3 - a real VT100 host
# could legitimately send these same byte values meaning their standard
# C0/control function, and KomportEmulation's slotReceivedChar() only
# special-cases BEL/BS/HT/LF/CR/ESC, so anything else falls straight
# into charset translation regardless of what a real device intended):
# - 0x00-0x1A (except 0x60/0x7B below): ATASCII's own graphics glyphs
#   for this range never collide with Atari's OWN control codes (Atari
#   keeps its 16 control functions cleanly in separate byte positions -
#   0x1B, 0x1C-0x1F, 0x7D-0x7F, 0x9B-0x9F, 0xFD-0xFF), but a *different*
#   real serial host speaking plain ASCII/VT100 could still send these
#   same bytes meaning VT/FF/SO/SI/DLE/etc. - same conflict class as
#   CP437, excluded for the same reason.
# - 0x1B, 0x1C-0x1F, 0x7D-0x7F, 0x9B-0x9F, 0xFD-0xFF: ATASCII's own 16
#   control characters (Escape, cursor movement, Clear Screen, Delete,
#   Tab, End-of-Line, Delete/Insert Line, Tab stops, Buzzer, Delete/
#   Insert Character) - genuine control functions, not display
#   characters, excluded for the same reason as 0x00-0x1A above. Note
#   on 0x9B specifically: Wikipedia's own table nominally lists it as
#   "U+000A LINE FEED", but KomportEmulation's control-code switch
#   matches the *raw* incoming byte (0x9B), not a charset-translated
#   result - mapping 0x9B here would not actually produce a working
#   line break, only an invisible character occupying one cell, so it
#   is excluded as non-functional rather than merely risky.
# - 0x80-0xFF, all entries not listed below: ATASCII's "inverse video"
#   range is almost entirely just the 0x00-0x7F glyphs redrawn in
#   inverse colours, not a distinct character - not representable in
#   this byte-to-single-Unicode-codepoint format at all (and mapping
#   them to the same codepoint as their non-inverse original would
#   only create an ambiguous byte<->character collision, triggering
#   this project's own duplicate-mapping warning, for no real gain).
#   The entries below are the exception: genuine distinct glyphs whose
#   visual inverse happens to already be a separate standard Unicode
#   character in its own right (e.g. the inverse of an upper-left
#   triangle is visually a lower-right triangle, a different glyph).
#
# The two ASCII-range substitutions (backtick and left-brace replaced
# by card-suit symbols):
60=2666
7B=2660
# 0x80-0xFF entries with a genuinely distinct Unicode code point:
82=258A
88=25E4
89=259B
8A=25E5
8B=2599
8C=259F
8D=2586
# 0x8E and 0x96 are also genuinely distinct glyphs (U+1FB85 "upper
# three quarters block", U+1FB8A "right three quarters block"), but
# both code points are above 0xFFFF - this file format has no astral/
# surrogate-pair support at all (see komportcharset.cpp's
# loadCustomCharsetFile(), "no surrogate-pair/astral support"), so
# they cannot be represented here and are left as identity instead.
8F=259C
94=25D8
95=2580
99=2590
